Id: lin040w
Severity: WARN
Category: kernel
Check: check_secureboot
Fix: Add lockdown=integrity to GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX and regenerate the GRUB configuration.
References: kernel_lockdown(7)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST CM-14, SI-7(9); ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

Secure Boot is on, but the running kernel is not locked down. Secure
Boot guarantees only that the kernel which started was signed; without
lockdown, root can still change it while it runs: load unsigned
modules (unless module.sig_enforce is set), kexec an unsigned kernel,
write kernel memory through /dev/mem, or reach the hardware directly
(I/O ports, model-specific registers). Malware with root can then hide
in the kernel, and Secure Boot no longer says anything about what is
running.

Lockdown in "integrity" mode blocks these; "confidentiality" also
keeps root from reading kernel memory. Debian, Ubuntu and Fedora build
their kernels to switch to integrity when Secure Boot is on
(CONFIG_LOCK_DOWN_IN_EFI_SECURE_BOOT). Elsewhere, add lockdown=integrity
to the kernel command line (GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX in /etc/default/grub,
then update the GRUB configuration). Lockdown also turns off
hibernation and some debugging and tracing tools, which is the price.
